<h1>Knicks Are <em>2026 NBA Champions</em></h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📅 June 13, 2026</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏀 New York Knicks</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏆 Brunson Named Finals MVP</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    The New York Knicks are NBA champions for the first time since 1973. Jalen Brunson — the point guard whose wingspan ranked 1,679th out of 1,812 players ever measured at the combine — is the Finals MVP. Fifty-three years of waiting ended with the player who, by every physical measurement that supposedly predicts NBA success, should never have been here.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">
<h2>The Finals, Game by Game — How It Happened</h2>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Game</th>
<th>Date</th>
<th>Result</th>
<th>Brunson Stats</th>
<th>The Story</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="highlight"><td>Game 1</td><td>June 3</td><td>Knicks 105-95 W</td><td>30 pts, 2 ast</td><td>Played through injury, 13 in Q4</td></tr>
<tr class="highlight"><td>Game 2</td><td>June 5</td><td>Knicks 105-104 W</td><td>28 pts</td><td>Won on the road by 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Game 3</td><td>June 9</td><td>Spurs 115-111 L</td><td>32 pts, 5 ast</td><td>Found his shot at MSG, not enough</td></tr>
<tr class="highlight"><td>Game 4</td><td>June 10</td><td>Knicks 107-106 W</td><td>36 pts, 7 ast, 3 stl</td><td>Down 29 — greatest comeback in Finals history</td></tr>
<tr class="highlight"><td>Game 5</td><td>June 13</td><td>Knicks W — Champions</td><td>45 pts, 3 reb, 3 ast</td><td>45-point masterpiece on the road — first Knicks title since 1973</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Every game in this series was decided late — until Game 5. With a championship on the line on the road in San Antonio, Brunson delivered the signature performance of the entire Finals: 45 points, 3 rebounds, and 3 assists, by far the highest-scoring game of the series for either team. Four of the first five games had been within four points in the final two minutes — the kind of series that should, by any reasonable basketball logic, have gone to the team with the 12-foot-8-inch peak reach and the Defensive Player of the Year at center. Instead, the Knicks closed it out behind the same player who carried them through the entire postseason: a 6-foot-1 point guard whose combine profile sits in the bottom 10 percent of nearly 1,800 players ever tested.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🏆</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>53 years.</strong> The Knicks' last championship came in 1973, defeating the Lakers. Their only other title was 1970. Two championships in 80 years of franchise history — and the third came on a 45-point closeout from a player who was the 33rd pick in the 2018 draft, passed over by 32 teams partly because of the exact measurements broken down in this article. Forty-five points to win a championship on the road is not a career-defining performance — it is the kind of game players talk about for the rest of their lives.</div>
</div>
<h2>The Player Ranked 1,679th All Time Just Won a Championship</h2>
<div class="player-card">
<div class="player-name">Jalen Brunson</div>
<div class="player-sub">New York Knicks · Point Guard · 2026 NBA Champion · Finals MVP · Age 29</div>
<div class="player-stats">
<div class="player-stat below">
<div class="player-stat-val">6'1"</div>
<div class="player-stat-label">Height — 1,617th all time</div>
</div>
